            <a href="#trueform-trainer" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">TrueForm Trainer</a>
            <a href="#trueform-trainer" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Facility Pick:</span>
            <a href="#trueform-runner" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">TrueForm Runner</a>
            <a href="#trueform-runner" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Cheaper Rival:</span>
            <a href="#assaultrunner-pro" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">AssaultRunner Pro</a>
            <a href="#assaultrunner-pro" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Value:</span>
            <a href="#sole-c80" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">SOLE C80 Curved Manual Treadmill</a>
            <a href="#sole-c80" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Should You Buy a TrueForm Treadmill? The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#who-makes" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Who Makes TrueForm, and How It Sells</a>
          <a href="#curve" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Makes a TrueForm Different: the Shallow Curve</a>
          <a href="#runner-vs-trainer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. TrueForm Runner vs Trainer: the Figures, Checked</a>
          <a href="#machines" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. The TrueForm Machines, and Two Cheaper Rivals</a>
          <a href="#runner-options" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Runner Options: Treads, Displays, Enduro and the Performance Adapter</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. The TrueForm Warranty, Read Properly</a>
          <a href="#space-delivery" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Space, Floors and Delivery</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Who a TrueForm Suits, and Who Should Buy Something Else</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you have searched for a TrueForm treadmill you have probably seen one in a pro team's
          training video or a physical-therapy clinic before you saw a price. TrueForm builds curved,
          non-motorized treadmills whose belt moves only when you push it, and it markets them on one
          idea: that a very shallow curve rewards good running form and punishes bad form, rather than
          pulling you along. There are two machines that matter. The Runner is hand-built in
          Wisconsin, highly customizable and costs from about $6,200 to over $10,000. The Trainer is a
          lighter, cheaper design built overseas, at $3,495 to $3,995 on the day we checked. This guide
          sets out what each one is, which figures the sources actually agree on, what the warranty
          covers, and whether either one is worth the money against the cheaper curved trainers most
          home buyers end up choosing.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Should You Buy a TrueForm Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a TrueForm Trainer if you want a premium curved manual treadmill with a 400 lb
            rating and a calmer, shallower curve than most rivals, and you are happy to pay about
            $1,000 more than an AssaultRunner Pro for it. Buy the Runner only if you are equipping a
            facility, a team or a serious athlete, and the custom treads and heavier build matter more
            than the price.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            On 5 November 2026 TrueForm's US store listed the TRUEFORM.TRAINER at $3,495 with a rubber
            tread and $3,995 with turf, against a stated compare-at price of $3,999, and said it ships in
            one to three business days. Rogue Fitness, one of TrueForm's dealers, had the Trainer at the
            same $3,495. The TRUEFORM.RUNNER ran from $6,195 to $10,195 across 45 combinations of frame
            colour, tread and display, with a 14-week build time; its standard configuration, black frame
            with Kraiburg rubber tread and the standard display, was $7,495.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those are high prices for a machine without a motor, an incline, a screen of any size worth
            the name or, on the Trainer, a resistance setting. What you are paying for is a particular
            running feel, a heavy steel build and a long frame warranty. If those matter, a TrueForm is a
            defensible buy. If you mostly want a curved deck for intervals and sprints in a garage gym, the
            <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>AssaultRunner Pro</a> and SOLE C80 do that job for much less, and our
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best curved treadmills</a>
            roundup compares all of them on one page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Makes TrueForm, and How It Sells</h2>
<p>
            TrueForm is a US company whose Runner is hand-built at its own facility in Wisconsin. Its
            store, trueformrunner.com, sells three treadmill listings — the Runner, the Enduro (a
            pared-back Runner with lower handrails and no display) and the Trainer — plus belts,
            displays, chargers and a sprint accessory called the Performance Adapter. TrueForm's own
            comparison page is candid about the split: the Runner is "Built in the USA", while the
            Trainer is "Designed and Engineered in house" and "Built in our manufacturing facility
            overseas."
          </p>
          <p>
            You will not find TrueForm on Amazon. It sells direct and through strength-equipment dealers
            such as Rogue Fitness and Sorinex, which is why most of the reviews you will read come from
            garage-gym and coaching sites rather than general retailers. That has two practical
            consequences. First, prices barely move: there are no lightning deals, and the discounts we
            saw were TrueForm's own, mirrored by its dealers. Second, freight delivery, returns and
            warranty claims run through TrueForm or the dealer you bought from, so read their terms
            before you order rather than assuming the generous return windows of a big-box retailer.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a>
            covers what to ask about freight.
          </p>
          <p>
            A note on names. The search terms people use — "trueform.runner", "TrueForm Trainer",
            "TrueForm Performance" — map onto TrueForm's own branding in slightly confusing ways.
            TRUEFORM.RUNNER is the flagship. TRUEFORM.TURF, TRUEFORM.TRACK and the Naboso model are the
            Runner with a different tread, sold as options on the same listing. "Performance" is not a
            separate treadmill: it is one of TrueForm's three use categories (alongside wellness and
            functional training) and the name of a $300 tethering accessory. The Trainer is the only
            genuinely different machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curve" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Makes a TrueForm Different: the Shallow Curve</h2>
<p>
            Every curved manual treadmill works the same way at heart. The running surface is a belt of
            slats on bearings, shaped so the front rises. Step onto the front of the curve and gravity
            and your stride push the belt backwards; drift to the back and it slows. There is no motor
            setting a pace, so speed is whatever you produce. Our
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>
            explains the mechanics in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where makers differ is the depth of the curve. A deep curve gives you more help: lean
            forward and the belt runs away with you, which feels exciting in a sprint and twitchy at a
            steady jog. TrueForm's pitch is the opposite. It describes its curve as the least steep on
            the market, gives the Trainer's as a 2.5-inch shallow curve, and says the symmetrical curve at
            the rear gives you control that flat-backed rivals lack. Independent reviewers broadly agree
            that a TrueForm feels calmer and less eager than an AssaultRunner, and that holding a steady
            pace takes more deliberate effort. TreadmillReviews.com reports that the Runner's belt is
            heavier than the Trainer's, which makes the Runner harder to keep moving.
          </p>
          <p>
            TrueForm goes further, with a feature it calls Responsive Belt Technology: in its words,
            "poor posture and form result in the belt moving slowly, while proper posture and form move
            the belt with ease." That is a plausible description of how a shallow, heavy curve behaves,
            and coaches who like TrueForm use it as a form-feedback tool. TrueForm's site also cites a
            gait study and a "33% higher calorie burn" than on a traditional treadmill. We have not seen
            the full study or the calorie test, so treat those as the maker's claims, not established
            findings. What is reasonably well supported is that self-powered running at a given pace is
            harder work than on a motorized belt; our
            <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorized treadmill</a>
            comparison covers why.
          </p>
          <p>
            The flip side is that a shallow curve is less forgiving for slow walking. One independent
            reviewer argues the Trainer suits running far better than desk walking, and that matches the
            general rule that the gentler the curve, the more effort a slow pace takes to sustain. If most
            of your use will be walking, see the section on alternatives below.
          </p>
        </div>

