          <p>
            Two things stand out. The first is how much of the difference is about build rather than
            dimensions: on Rogue's listings the two machines share the same 54 by 17-inch running surface
            and the same 64 by 31 by 63-inch footprint, and the Runner's extra money buys heavier-gauge
            steel, a heavier belt, US assembly, more tread choices and a longer warranty on labour and
            electronics. The second is that both surfaces are 17 inches wide. That is normal for curved
            trainers — the AssaultRunner Pro is also 17 inches — but narrow next to a 22-inch motorized
            deck, and broad-shouldered runners notice it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Trainer was redesigned recently. TrueForm calls the current one "all new" and lists
            upgraded treads and rollers rated to 400 lb at top speed, a front Power Bar for sprint starts,
            modular mounts for future attachments, and Bluetooth that connects to FTMS apps such as <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a>
            and Strava. Reviews from 2023 describe the earlier version, which explains why the machine
            weight differs between sources. If you buy a Trainer second-hand, ask which version it is.
          </p>
        </div>

