      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="runner-options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Runner Options: Treads, Displays, Enduro and the Performance Adapter</h2>
<p>
            The Runner's price spread comes almost entirely from three choices. On 5 November 2026
            TrueForm's listing priced them as follows.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Tread.</strong> Kraiburg rubber is the standard surface. Green or black turf,
            red athletic track and a textured Naboso surface each added about $700 to the rubber price
            in a black or white frame. Turf suits sled pushes and cleated athletes; Naboso is aimed at
            barefoot training.</li>
            <li><strong>Display.</strong> The standard display is battery-powered and shows pace,
            distance, time and speed. TrueTouch adds a touchscreen with Bluetooth, HDMI and built-in apps;
            Rogue and Sorinex list Netflix and Zwift among them. It added $1,000 on most configurations.
            The cheapest option on the Runner listing, labelled "Enduro", drops the display and was
            $1,300 below the standard display.</li>
            <li><strong>Frame colour.</strong> Black or white powder coat, or a custom RAL colour for
            branding a facility, which added between $700 and $1,000.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            TrueForm also lists the TRUEFORM.ENDURO as its own product: the Runner's base with lower
            handrails and no display, which TrueForm says is designed to let you focus on movement. Its
            separate listing showed $5,995 in white with rubber and $6,495 to $6,995 in black, which does
            not line up exactly with the $6,195 "Enduro" option on the Runner listing. We could not find
            independent coverage of the Enduro to cross-check, so ask TrueForm which price applies to the
            configuration you want before you order.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Performance Adapter is a $300 accessory ($400 in a custom colour) that TrueForm describes
            as a tethering system that "allows athletes to add tension for high-intensity workouts",
            designed for explosive acceleration work. It fits the Runner, Turf, Track and office versions,
            not the Trainer. Neither TrueForm machine has a built-in resistance setting in the way that
            magnetic-brake trainers such as the SOLE C80 and the
            <a href="/bells-of-steel-blitz-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bells of Steel Blitz</a>
            do, so if resisted sprints and sled-style pushes are central to your training, that is a real
            difference to weigh.
          </p>
          <p>
            Replacement belts are expensive, which is worth knowing before you choose a tread: TrueForm's
            parts store listed a Runner Kraiburg belt at $2,295, turf and track belts at $2,549 and a
            Naboso belt at $2,675. These are long-life parts and most home owners will never replace one,
            but a facility with heavy use should budget for it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The TrueForm Warranty, Read Properly</h2>
<p>
            TrueForm's warranty page, read on 5 November 2026, gives the Runner a lifetime frame
            warranty, five years on moving parts, five years on electronics and the display, and two
            years of labour. The Trainer has the same lifetime frame and five years on moving parts, but
            three years on electronics and one year of labour. The page adds that coverage varies by
            model, component, usage environment and purchase date, and points to a full warranty
            document for the binding terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions. Older coverage quotes different terms: TreadmillReviews.com's 2024 review gives
            the Runner a 20-year frame warranty, and Garage Gym Lab's 2023 review of the earlier Trainer
            describes five years on the frame, parts and components. Those may reflect older policies or
            older machines, but they are a reminder to get the warranty for your purchase in writing.
            And "lifetime" frame cover is less valuable than it sounds on a machine with no motor and few
            moving parts; the parts that wear on a curved trainer are the bearings, the slats and the belt,
            so the five-year moving-parts term is the one that matters. On that measure TrueForm is
            competitive: Assault lists three years on the AssaultRunner Pro's moving parts, and SOLE gives
            the C80 one year on parts. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to compare terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for TrueForm on
            5 November 2026 and found no recalls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Space, Floors and Delivery</h2>
<p>
            Rogue lists both machines at 64 inches long, 31 wide and 63 high, which is shorter than most
            motorized running treadmills, and neither needs a power outlet. Add clearance behind the deck
            for safety; our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill space guide</a>
            gives the usual allowances. Neither machine folds.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weight is the bigger planning problem. Rogue gives the Trainer a 300 lb product weight and a
            326 lb shipping weight in a 73 by 32 by 24-inch crate, and lists the Runner at 350 lb. Both
            ship by freight to the 48 contiguous states with no pickup from Rogue, so plan the route from
            the kerb to the room: doorways, stairs and whether the carrier will bring it inside. A
            ground-floor garage or basement with a level concrete floor and a heavy rubber mat is the ideal
            home; see our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mat picks</a>
            and the notes on <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running a treadmill in a garage</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Lead time is the other difference. The Trainer was listed as shipping in one to three business
            days by TrueForm and Rogue. The Runner is built to order: TrueForm said 14 weeks, Rogue six to
            eight weeks and Sorinex up to ten to twelve. If you need it for a season start or a facility
            opening, confirm the date in writing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who a TrueForm Suits, and Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A TrueForm Trainer suits</strong> a heavier runner who wants a curved trainer rated to
            400 lb, a runner or coach who values the calmer shallow curve for steady running and form
            work, and a home gym that wants a premium machine without a 14-week wait. It is also the
            sensible TrueForm for a family: the Power Bar gives children a low grip point, and the lighter
            belt is easier to get moving.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A TrueForm Runner suits</strong> a performance facility, a team, a clinic or a very
            committed athlete, where heavy daily use, custom branding and turf or track treads justify the
            price. For a home runner it is far more machine than needed, and the money would buy a curved
            trainer and a good motorized treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy something else if</strong> you mainly want to walk (a motorized treadmill or a
            walking pad is easier; see our <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual treadmill guide</a>
            for the flat, cheap end), if you want built-in resistance for sled pushes (the SOLE C80 and
            the Bells of Steel Blitz have magnetic brakes), or if you simply want the curved-treadmill
            experience at the lowest price that is still well built, which is the AssaultRunner Pro's job.
            For workouts that suit any curved deck, see our
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

